# Line endings are part of this project's contract, not a preference.
#
# src/constants/text/*.txt is embedded verbatim with include_str! and ends up
# in the system prompt and the tool descriptions sent to the model. A Windows
# clone with core.autocrlf=true checked those files out as CRLF, so the binary
# shipped prompts whose every line ended \r\n while the code that composes the
# surrounding text used \n. That broke the byte-for-byte rule against the
# Python oracle (README, coding rule 1) and failed four prompt fixture tests.
#
# eol=lf pins the working tree to LF on every platform, which is what the index
# already stores. After changing this file, run `git add --renormalize .` once
# on a clone that was checked out with CRLF.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary: never translate, never diff as text.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.exe binary
# The POSIX ripgrep binaries have no extension, so nothing above matches them
# and they fall to `* text=auto`, i.e. to git's content heuristic. That happens
# to answer "binary" today, but a 5MB executable should not depend on a guess:
# a wrong answer would run eol=lf over it and corrupt it on checkout.
vendor/ripgrep/*/rg binary
*.dll binary
*.so binary
*.dylib binary

# cmd.exe needs CRLF. With the LF working tree the global rule above produced,
# `setup.bat` ran as garbage on a fresh clone: cmd tracks its position in a
# batch file assuming \r\n and, on LF-only input, resumes each line a couple of
# bytes late -- `setlocal` became `tlocal`, `echo` became `ho`, and the script
# died at line 2. This rule is more specific than `*`, so it wins for .bat.
*.bat text eol=crlf
